From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Joshua Warren <address-in-upstream-commit@localhost>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 09:53:04 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] soc: apple: aop: Send the 14-inch M5 Max sensor its 193-byte
 calibration

The 14-inch M5 Max firmware asks for a setup buffer on the ALS endpoint
(0x21) and does not start the sensor until that endpoint has had its
calibration. The driver only took T8140's 80-byte message, and the board
named no calibration file, so the step was skipped ("the sensor stays
off") and every illuminance read returned ENODATA.

On that board the firmware accepts a 216-byte message instead: the
operation code, the blob length 193, the sensor's 193-byte blob and
zeros, answered with status 0. 56-byte bodies and a zero length were
rejected with 0xc2. Build that message from the 193-byte file
(als_calibration_message, a pure function) and name the file in the
K114c device tree only: the M5 Pro firmware never asks for the endpoint.

Test: tools/asahi/aop-als-calibration-tests.py checks the 80-byte
pass-through, the 216-byte layout and the refused sizes; fourteen mutants
of the builder are killed. On a 14-inch M5 Max booted with the driver change and the same
firmware-name property in its device tree (a hand-built board DTB, not the
tree's t6050-k114c.dtb), in_illuminance_input read 12.2 to 12.8 lux for
36 seconds with no ENODATA and no warning-level aop lines.

Signed-off-by: Joshua Warren <address-in-upstream-commit@localhost>
(cherry picked from commit 242ce586c508bcdccc2a7031f02a72ca661e22f3)
---
 arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t6050-k114c.dts | 9 +++++++++
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t6050-k114c.dts b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t6050-k114c.dts
index ed1f3ec3036a..a32f9f9bea5f 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t6050-k114c.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t6050-k114c.dts
@@ -339,6 +339,15 @@ &aop {
 	status = "okay";
 };
 
+/*
+ * This board's AOP firmware asks for the sensor's setup buffer and starts the sensor only once the
+ * setup port has had its calibration: the 193-byte blob the driver sends again as the sensor's
+ * property later. Boards whose firmware never asks (the 14-inch M5 Pro) must not name one.
+ */
+&aop_als {
+	firmware-name = "apple/aop-als-cal.bin";
+};
+
 /*
  * The microphone array: ADT audio-hp-mic, three microphones on leap-s RX0 through the
  * proxy ADMAC (Linux channel 1, as on J813), captured by the AOP audio service's own card.
